#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097862 :tutorial/content "The **linear correlation coefficient,** denoted $\\rho$ (the Greek letter \"rho\"), is a number that gives the strength of the *linear* dependence between two sets of paired observations $(x,y)$.\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient satisfies the following constraint:\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe interpret the endpoints of this interval as follows:\n\n- $\\rho = 1$ means that there is a perfect *positive* correlation between the data points.\n- $\\rho = -1$ means that there is a perfect *negative* correlation between the data points.\n\nMoreover, $\\rho = 0$ means there is no *linear* correlation between the data points.\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is **strong,** and the data points closely follow a straight line.![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)\n\n- If $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is **weak.** The data points follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)\n\n- If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is **negligible.** There is no (linear) correlation.![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "372f5b4c-9826-52f6-a70c-5e3a035de64e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3699 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097891 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\big\\{y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\nof the same size.\n\nLet's now introduce the following notation:\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \\qquad S_{yy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i-\\overline{y})^2, \\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y})\n$$\n\nNotice that $S_{xx}, S_{yy}$, and $S_{xy}$ are the sums of square deviations found in the formulas for the variance of $x$, the variance of $y$, and covariance of $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nUsing the previous definition, we can show that the correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ab8695a-49cb-5bc1-8934-ffd2ff28261e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5966 :tutorial/title "The Linear Correlation Coefficient in Sigma Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097902 :tutorial/content "To calculate $\\rho$, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance between $x$ and $y$, and\n- $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nFor example, consider data sets $x$ and $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)=-9, \\qquad \\sigma_x=6, \\qquad \\sigma_y=2\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ(x, y) &= ((-9))/((6) \\cdot (2)) \\\\\n&= ((-9))/(12) \\\\\n&=-0.75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho$ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, there is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y$." :tutorial/id #uuid "6f296964-c1ff-5379-861d-1a9809ca7cc6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6586 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Correlation Coefficient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097903 :tutorial/content "Let's derive the following result:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of the correlation coefficient $\\rho$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac1n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}) = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{n}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ_{x} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx}}{n}} \\\\\nσ_{y} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)(y_{i} - y^(―))^{2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{yy}}{n}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our definition for $\\rho$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= (Cov(x, y))/(σ_{x} \\cdot σ_{y}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx}}{n}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{S_{yy}}{n}}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}{n^{2}}}) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}{n})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{S_{xy}}{n}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}{n})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "219dd7c3-97f9-5504-ad97-d8710a6cf5bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6587 :tutorial/title "Proving the Formula for Rho in Terms of Squared Deviations"}]}
